.TH ESDM\-CUSE\-RANDOM 8 "2026-08-05" "ESDM @VERSION@" "ESDM Manual"
.SH NAME
esdm\-cuse\-random, esdm\-cuse\-urandom \- serve /dev/random and /dev/urandom
from the ESDM
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B esdm\-cuse\-random
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.br
.B esdm\-cuse\-urandom
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
These two daemons provide the Linux character devices
.I /dev/random
and
.I /dev/urandom
from
.BR esdm\-server (8)
instead of from the kernel RNG, using the CUSE (character device in userspace)
facility of FUSE.
.PP
Each daemon registers a CUSE device of its own \-
.I /dev/esdm
for
.B esdm\-cuse\-random
and
.I /dev/uesdm
for
.B esdm\-cuse\-urandom
\- which is then bind\-mounted over the well\-known device path. Applications
therefore keep using
.I /dev/random
and
.I /dev/urandom
unchanged, including the
.BR ioctl (2)
commands
.BR RNDGETENTCNT ,
.BR RNDADDTOENTCNT ,
.BR RNDADDENTROPY ,
.B RNDZAPENTCNT/RNDCLEARPOOL
and
.BR RNDRESEEDCRNG ,
and including
.BR poll (2)
on the write side.
.PP
.B esdm\-cuse\-random
serves the blocking semantics: reads wait until the ESDM is fully seeded.
.B esdm\-cuse\-urandom
serves the non\-blocking semantics.
.PP
Both daemons must be started as root and drop to an unprivileged user after
the device has been created. On systemd installations they are normally
started through the shipped
.BR esdm\-cuse\-random.service " and " esdm\-cuse\-urandom.service
units, which are pulled in by
.BR esdm\-linux\-compat.target .
.PP
The options are handled by FUSE, so the general FUSE and CUSE options are
accepted in addition to the ones listed below.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.BR \-M ", " \-\-maj= \fIMAJ\fR
Major number of the device to create.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-min= \fIMIN\fR
Minor number of the device to create.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-name= \fINAME\fR
Name of the device to create below
.IR /dev .
Mandatory.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbosity= \fINUM\fR
Logging verbosity level.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-username " \fIUSER\fR"
Unprivileged user to switch to once the device exists. Defaults to
.BR nobody .
.TP
.B \-\-pid_namespace
Fork the daemon into an isolating PID namespace.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " "\-o debug"
Enable debug output. Implies
.BR \-f .
.TP
.B \-f
Run in the foreground.
.TP
.B \-s
Disable multi\-threaded operation.
.TP
.BR \-S ", " \-\-syslog
Log via syslog.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /dev/esdm
CUSE device registered by
.BR esdm\-cuse\-random .
.TP
.I /dev/uesdm
CUSE device registered by
.BR esdm\-cuse\-urandom .
.SH NOTES
Before each daemon overlays the kernel device with its own, it keeps a file
descriptor on the original kernel
.IR /dev/random " respectively " /dev/urandom
open. Should a request to
.BR esdm\-server (8)
fail, the daemon serves it from that descriptor rather than failing the
.BR read (2)
or
.BR write (2),
so that a stopped or crashed ESDM does not take the system's random device
down with it.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR esdm\-server (8),
.BR esdm\-proc (8),
.BR esdm\-kernel\-seeder (8),
.BR random (4),
.BR cuse (4)
.SH AUTHOR
Stephan Mueller <smueller@chronox.de>
